[\\log_{10}(15) = 1.1761\\]\n\\[\\log_{10}(14) = 1.1461\\]\n\\[\\log_{10}(13) = 1.1139\\]\n\\[\\log_{10}(12) = 1.0792\\]\n\\[\\log_{10}(11) = 1.0414\\]\n\\[\\log_{10}(10) = 1.0000\\]\n\\[\\log_{10}(9) = 0.9542\\]\n\\[\\log_{10}(8) = 0.9031\\]\n\\[\\log_{10}(7) = 0.8451\\]\n\\[\\log_{10}(6) = 0.7782\\]\n\\[\\log_{10}(5) = 0.6990\\]\n\\[\\log_{10}(4) = 0.6021\\]\n\\[\\log_{10}(3) = 0.4771\\]\n\\[\\log_{10}(2) = 0.3010\\]\n\\[\\log_{10}(1) = 0\\]\n\nSumming these:\n\n\\[\\log_{10}(20!) \\approx 1.3010 + 1.2788 + 1.2553 + 1.2304 + 1.2041 + 1.1761 + 1.1461 + 1.1139 + 1.0792 + 1.0414 + 1.0000 + 0.9542 + 0.9031 + 0.8451 + 0.7782 + 0.6990 + 0.6021 + 0.4771 + 0.3010 + 0\\]\n\\[= 18.3848\\]\n\nThis result means \\(20!\\) is close to \\(10^{18.3848}\\). The exponent \\(18.3848\\) can be split into \\(18\\) and \\(0.3848\\). The \\(10^{18}\\) part gives us the number of digits minus one, and \\(10^{0.3848}\\) gives us the leading digit. \n\nUsing a calculator, \\(10^{0.3848} \\approx 2.41\\).\n\nThus, the first digit of \\(20!\\) is \\(\\boxed{2}\\)."},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{2}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true,"retains_student_answer":false},"graded_text":"Given that \\(20!\\) has 19 digits and the last 18 digits are \\(432902008176640000\\), we understand that the last digit of \\(20!\\) is 0, as expected since factorials of numbers greater than 4 always end in 0 due to the presence of at least one factor of 10 (2 and 5).\n\nTo find the first digit, we need to approximate the value of \\(20!\\). We can use logarithms to do this effectively.\n\n\\[20! = 20 \\times 19 \\times 18 \\times \\cdots \\times 2 \\times 1\\]\n\nTaking the logarithm (base 10) of both sides:\n\n\\[\\log_{10}(20!) = \\log_{10}(20 \\times 19 \\times 18 \\times \\cdots \\times 2 \\times 1)\\]\n\\[= \\log_{10}(20) + \\log_{10}(19) + \\log_{10}(18) + \\cdots + \\log_{10}(2) + \\log_{10}(1)\\]\n\nUsing known logarithm values:\n\n\\[\\log_{10}(20) = 1.3010\\]\n\\[\\log_{10}(19) = 1.2788\\]\n\\[\\log_{10}(18) = 1.2553\\]\n\\[\\log_{10}(17) = 1.2304\\]\n\\[\\log_{10}(16) = 1.2041\\]\n\\[\\log_{10}(15) = 1.1761\\]\n\\[\\log_{10}(14) = 1.1461\\]\n\\[\\log_{10}(13) = 1.1139\\]\n\\[\\log_{10}(12) = 1.0792\\]\n\\[\\log_{10}(11) = 1.0414\\]\n\\[\\log_{10}(10) = 1.0000\\]\n\\[\\log_{10}(9) = 0.9542\\]\n\\[\\log_{10}(8) = 0.9031\\]\n\\[\\log_{10}(7) = 0.8451\\]\n\\[\\log_{10}(6) = 0.7782\\]\n\\[\\log_{10}(5) = 0.6990\\]\n\\[\\log_{10}(4) = 0.6021\\]\n\\[\\log_{10}(3) = 0.4771\\]\n\\[\\log_{10}(2) = 0.3010\\]\n\\[\\log_{10}(1) = 0\\]\n\nSumming these:\n\n\\[\\log_{10}(20!) \\approx 1.3010 + 1.2788 + 1.2553 + 1.2304 + 1.2041 + 1.1761 + 1.1461 + 1.1139 + 1.0792 + 1.0414 + 1.0000 + 0.9542 + 0.9031 + 0.8451 + 0.7782 + 0.6990 + 0.6021 + 0.4771 + 0.3010 + 0\\]\n\\[= 18.3848\\]\n\nThis result means \\(20!\\) is close to \\(10^{18.3848}\\). The exponent \\(18.3848\\) can be split into \\(18\\) and \\(0.3848\\). The \\(10^{18}\\) part gives us the number of digits minus one, and \\(10^{0.3848}\\) gives us the leading digit. \n\nUsing a calculator, \\(10^{0.3848} \\approx 2.41\\).\n\nThus, the first digit of \\(20!\\) is \\(\\boxed{2}\\).","id":"number_theory:445"}
